= Probability distribution — Technical =

== Induced measure ==
If X is a random variable on probability space (Ω,F,P), the distribution of X is the pushforward measure:

<pre>
P_X(B) = P(X ∈ B)
</pre>

for measurable sets B.

== Discrete case ==
A probability mass function satisfies:

<pre>
p_X(x) = P(X=x)
Σ_x p_X(x) = 1
</pre>

== Continuous case ==
If absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure:

<pre>
P_X(B) = ∫_B f_X(x) dx
</pre>

The density f_X is a Radon–Nikodym derivative, not a point probability.

== CDF ==
For real-valued X:

<pre>
F_X(x)=P(X≤x)
</pre>

The CDF is nondecreasing, right-continuous, and approaches 0/1 at the tails.

== Model families ==
Parametric families index distributions by finite-dimensional parameters; nonparametric methods relax that structure.

